+## Reproduction
 
-## Common input domain and episode construction
-
-The sampler uses one common domain for all settings. Both dimensions are 9..18;
-there are 4..8 markers, at most one per orthogonal lane. Every marker's axis
-coordinate lies in `3..extent-4`, leaving two-cell clearance. Markers appear on
-both non-tie sides; both orthogonal edge lanes also contain markers. This
-construction prevents overwriting a marker or border, prevents overlapping
-destinations, and makes every projection observable for every setting.
-
-These constraints are **stricter than the canonical input domain**. Canonical
-inputs closer to a border are still valid for the default canonical program,
-but are not promised to be valid distance-2 prototype samples. There is no
-parameter-specific input giveaway: `draw_input(seed, sample_index)` does not
-receive or look up the rule parameters.
-
-`choose_parameters(seed)` selects one of four settings using a domain-separated
-SHA-256 of the integer seed and prototype version. The selected values remain
-fixed across all three demonstrations and the one query. Layout draws use a
-different deterministic seed stream and leave Python's global RNG untouched.
-
-The four sample phases are odd horizontal, odd vertical, even horizontal, even
-vertical. Each consecutive four-pair episode therefore contains two odd-center
-witnesses, both border orientations, and both axis parities. In the production
-order (first three pairs are demonstrations), at least one odd witness remains.
-With any one pair held out, another odd witness remains among the three
-demonstrations. Every odd witness has a center marker and non-tie markers on
-separate lanes, so it distinguishes all four declared parameter settings.
-
-This is a constructive argument for this sampler and these four hypotheses.
-The retained execution evidence is finite. It does not establish unique
-identifiability against every conceivable ARC rule. “Rotation checks” here mean
-all four choices of held-out pair, as in the task's existing lesson tests;
-**geometric rotation equivariance is not claimed**. Coordinate-anchored tie
-choices would require corresponding treatment under geometric rotations.
-
-## Racket execution and the lesson gate
-
-`RacketRules` compiles the four exact programs through the included
-`arc_repl.racket_bridge.RacketBridge` and the shared `wrap_rule` normalizer.
-Every demonstration label, query label, contrast output, and parameter-rival
-prediction comes from that bridge. A Racket error propagates; there is no Python
-fallback. The independent `python_oracle` exists only for semantic checks in the
-focused tests and does not author labels.
-
-For a source snapshot, a task-local import adapter points the `scripts`
-namespace at that same snapshot's `shared/tools/` so the normalizer can use
-its existing parser. A staged runtime already supplies `scripts/`. No shared
-module is edited or copied, and a foreign cached namespace is rejected.
-When a staged task-test snapshot contains both layouts, its runtime manifest
-and `data/canonical` take precedence over the mirrored `shared/` tree; a
-focused regression covers that precedence.
-
-Before drawing a query, `build_episode` checks each of the four declared
-hypotheses against all demonstration labels. It requires the chosen setting to
-be the sole survivor. The private proof records a disagreeing demonstration,
-changed cell coordinates, and output hashes for each eliminated rival. The
-negative test replaces the input sampler with lawful even-axis examples and
-verifies that the unresolved tie parameter raises `AmbiguousLesson` before a
-fourth input is requested.
-
-The pre-query gate is deliberately conservative. Parameter non-identifiability
-is not always answer ambiguity: both tie settings can survive even-only
-demonstrations and still give the same answer on an even-axis query. Because
-this prototype requires unique parameters before it draws a query, it would
-refuse that harmless case too. A future admission policy could instead allow
-all demonstration-consistent declared rivals when they agree on the actual
-query answer. That query-conditioned policy is a proposal, not implemented
-here. The existing `test_even_axis_has_no_tie_parameter_effect` checks the
-agreement boundary through Racket.
-
-The public challenge contains only `train` input/output pairs and `test` inputs.
-It has no seed, parameter names/values, program, proof, or query answer. Share
-only the `public/` directory. The rest of an evidence export is authoring data.
-Each private episode contains its exact seed, parameters, program/hash, full
-labels, and demonstration proof. The private shared-input contrast gives one
-lawful input with four distinct Racket outputs.
